{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2957","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2957","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2957\",\"parent_revision\":\"91f6c8fc4a7535c8550be5815205cbda2e0350e1\",\"root_revision\":\"04a63c2d05c0099adcc0d9a713bfd37b6d489ff37a5dfec673436492102279b0\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-18\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md`'s `## Input` section documents its expected input as transcribed content handed to it by the orchestrator: \\\"Item title: The backlog item title\\\", \\\"Item description: The full description text\\\", \\\"RT-ICA summary (optional): Pre-computed RT-ICA assessment from the orchestrator.\\\" It has `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view` in its tool list, but its own Input contract never instructs it to call that tool itself \u2014 it's written as a content-receiver, not a content-reader.\\n\\nA retrospective read of the other 5 groom-swarm agent files (`impact-analyst.md`, `fact-checker.md`, `classifier.md`, `rtica-assessor.md`, `alignment-analyst.md`) confirmed they are pull-based: their instructions direct them to read the item via `backlog_view`/`artifact_read` themselves rather than expecting the orchestrator to transcribe content into their prompt. `backlog-item-groomer.md` is the sole outlier.\\n\\n`skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md`'s \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section reinforces the same pattern from the orchestrator side: \\\"Input to groomer: item title, description, source, priority, plan address, RT-ICA assessment, Fact-Check verdicts, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis..., Impact Radius, Research section...\\\" \u2014 worded as a list of content to pass into the prompt, not a pointer for the agent to resolve itself.\\n\\nThis produced a real effect this session: the orchestrator, following that documented contract, wrote a groomer dispatch prompt that transcribed other agents' section findings (RT-ICA verdict, Fact-Check results, Impact Radius summary, a specific interpretive framing of a Design Intent Alignment finding) directly into the prompt instead of pointing the agent at the item and letting it read independently \u2014 which is the specific anti-pattern this repository's own project instructions warn against (\\\"Pass file paths, let agents read... Never transcribe file contents into prompts \u2014 it bypasses agent verification\\\"). Since dh is meant to run standalone in any repository, this defect reproduces regardless of whether the installing repo has an equivalent convention documented, because the push-contract is baked into the plugin's own agent file and workflow documentation.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 discovered live while grooming #2953 via /dh:work-backlog-item, confirmed via retrospective sweep of groom-workflow docs 2026-08-18\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-18\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **dh:backlog-item-groomer's documented input contract is push-based (transcribed content) while all 5 sibling groom-swarm agents are pull-based** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"c336ebf16c56d9d14c0b7168c2e331c0b35c4ae7b477013cbf2f3305b2dc506a\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPTo7gw\"}"}